--- body ---
## Context

git-erg brought tickets into the repository; the other half of the cycle
still lives on the forge. Review and merge happen on GitHub, and the review
thread is the one piece of the development cycle that *dies* outside git:
when a PR closes, its discussion stays hostage to the forge. A local
merge-request system closes the loop -- ticket, branch, review, merge, all in
git, the forge demoted to an optional mirror. This extends erg's declared
reason for being ("disconnected environments") to the whole cycle.

Maintainer decision (2026-08-17): the MR system is **orthogonal and
composable** to git-erg, not fused into it. This ticket records the design
vision, the comparison that grounded the decision, and what remains open.
Should the design outgrow a ticket body, the house form is a design
charter in docs/ (cf. `docs/erg-imagine-charter.md`); this ticket remains
the tracking record either way.

## The vision

Same design signature as erg: one object = one plain-text file, editable
without the tool; the tool is a convenience, never a dependency; state is a
verifiable property, not a header field. Applied to MRs, the third principle
gets a sharper translation:

**An MR's state is the DAG.** An MR is open iff its source branch is not an
ancestor of its target. `git merge-base --is-ancestor` *is* the status
field. Nothing to keep in sync between "what the register says" and "what
git says": the register is git. (The MR-side analogue of "no Status:
header" -- stronger, since here not even the file path carries state.)

File format `%dyne 0.1`, same three-section grammar as `%erg 0.1`:

    %dyne 0.1
    Title: Add retry logic for failed API requests
    Source: t0042-retry
    Target: main
    Created: 2026-08-17
    Author: minh
    Closes: 0042

    --- log ---
    2026-08-17T09:12Z minh opened
    2026-08-17T09:40Z ci pass sha=abc1234
    2026-08-17T10:02Z gaze verdict APPROVED sha=abc1234

    --- body ---
    ## What, why

(Settled 2026-08-26: the log lives inline, exactly as shown -- one file
per MR, same shape as a ticket. The log is one-liners; the body is the
discussion: prose, whose opening post is the author's description --
comment #0, as on a forge -- with later comments appended as
timestamp-attributed blocks. Named the same day: **dyne**, the CGS unit
of force -- erg = dyne x cm; an MR is a force applied to main, and the
work is done when main moves. Standalone binary `dyne` like `erg`,
extension `.dyne`, config `.dynerc`, store `merges/`.)

Settled design choices:

- **Home: on the source branch** (`merges/<branch>.dyne`). Opening an MR =
  committing the file to the branch. Merging integrates the file into main:
  arrival in main's history IS the archival act -- like erg's move to
  closed/, but free and atomic. Rejected: dedicated refs a la
  Gerrit/git-appraise (invisible in the tree, exactly what this lineage
  refuses); a shared registry branch (permanent conflicts under parallel
  sessions); fusing MR and ticket into one object (couples two lifecycles;
  ticketless MRs become second-class).
- **Multi-writer file, union merge** (home settled 2026-08-26). Reviewers
  and CI append from different clones; a plain append-only file conflicts
  on every double append. Git already has the needed CRDT: `merge=union`
  on `merges/*.dyne` in .gitattributes, from day one. Log and discussion
  are different things and each is union-safe by its own form: log
  entries are independent one-liners (reordered by timestamp at display
  time); discussion comments are contiguous prose blocks under a
  timestamp-attributed heading, appended, never rewritten (a review
  thread is append-only by nature), so a concurrent double append keeps
  both blocks and the headings recover the order. The residual union
  hazard -- two clones editing the same header or the same existing
  prose line merge silently, both lines kept -- is fenced by rarity
  (writers other than the author only append) and by `dyne check`
  hard-failing duplicate headers. Rejected: a sibling `.log` file (a
  second home for what the format already houses, and a dent in
  one-object-one-file); scoping union away by custom merge driver (clean
  merges must not depend on the tool being installed).
- **Identity: no number at open time.** An MR is identified by
  (Source, Target); the branch namespace is already unique, so the whole
  optimistic-allocation pathology (git-erg#282's collision class) has no object
  here. And none at merge time either (settled 2026-08-26): **the merge
  commit SHA is the MR's permanent name**. No counter, no allocator, no
  register-like artifact -- the register is git, all the way down. House
  citation style: `merged as <short-sha>`; the hook contract passes
  `{sha}`, not a serial. (Merge-time allocation would have been safe by
  construction -- merges to main are serialized by main itself -- but
  safe is not needed.)
- **Review entries are typed and sha-pinned**: `note`, `verdict
  APPROVED|REROLL|ESCALATE sha=`, `ci pass|fail sha=`, comment anchors
  `path:line@sha`. A verdict on a stale head becomes mechanically
  detectable: `dyne merge` refuses if the last verdict is not on HEAD. Review
  enters the project's long-term memory instead of dying with the forge PR.
- **Atomic merge**: `dyne merge` verifies verdict@HEAD and ci@HEAD, runs
  the hooks, stages everything they changed into the merge commit itself,
  merges --no-ff. The staging is recursive on the store (`add -A`, never
  `add -u`): a hook may rename files and edit files it was never named
  (see the erg close shape below), and a rename staged apart from its
  edit silently carries the pre-edit blob. Atomicity is the commit's: if
  a hook dies midway, the worktree holds its partial work and nothing is
  committed. The erg-pr-merge bounce pathology (ticket closed locally but
  never pushed, detached cherry-pick recovery recipes) exists because
  ticket-closing and merging live in two systems that can succeed
  separately; one commit dissolves it.

## Composed, not integrated -- the comparison

"Composed vs integrated" bundles three independent decisions:

- (a) **the spec** -- a `%dyne` format inside the erg spec, or autonomous;
- (b) **the behavior** -- native ticket-closing inside an `erg mr merge`, or
  opaque refs + exec hook;
- (c) **the implementation** -- one codebase/binary or two.

Most "integrated" advantages live in (c); most "composed" advantages live in
(a)+(b). Summary:

| Criterion | Integrated (`erg mr`) | Composed (`dyne`) |
|---|---|---|
| Initial cost (parser, validator, release reused) | wins | ~40% duplicated |
| Grammar drift between the two formats | wins (one parser) | real risk, by imitation |
| Maturity decoupling (erg spec is a 0.1 working draft) | versions chained | wins |
| MR-only adoption (repo without tickets; branding) | "install a ticket tool to get MRs" | wins |
| Cross-store integrity (Closes -> existing open ticket; two open MRs claiming one ticket) | wins: native validator rule | glue script |
| Seam risk (forgotten hook -> ticket silently never closed) | wins: native path | real, mitigable (see below) |
| Failure isolation | shared blast radius | wins |
| Agent mental model (bootstrap, doc, store discovery) | wins, slightly | two binaries, two docs |
| Scope-creep temptation ("the forge in one binary", Fossil trajectory) | structural | wins |

Seam-risk mitigation, expressible without knowing erg: `dyne merge` refuses
if `Closes:` is non-empty and no on-merge hook is configured.

**Verdict: composed on (a) and (b)** -- two specs, two stores, opaque refs,
exec hook. That is where conceptual orthogonality, separate adoption, and
the right to churn live; the MR side is the experimental half of the system,
so maturity decoupling matters most exactly there.

**(c) settled by the maintainer (2026-08-17): one codebase, two specs.**
dyne lives in this repository, sharing the parser, atomic-write and
validator machinery as internal modules. The two specs stay autonomous --
`erg spec` and `dyne spec`, each normative for its own files, neither
citing the other -- so the contracts remain orthogonal while the sharing
stays invisible to users. The table above is the pre-decision record;
under this settlement the grammar-drift row dies (one parser), the
initial-cost row is void (machinery shared), the failure-isolation row
flips to the shared blast radius the integrated column named, and the
agent-mental-model row reduces to "two docs" (one busybox blob at two
paths, not two binaries). Traded away: release-level decoupling (one
release cycle ships both tools). Kept: format-level decoupling (`%erg`
and `%dyne` version independently). Distribution note: an identical
busybox-style binary committed at `tickets/erg` and `merges/dyne` costs
one git blob (content-addressed), two checkout paths; argv[0] picks the
personality.

## The composition seam -- three layers, zero imports

1. **Grammar**, shared by convention, not by library. `%dyne 0.1` imitates the
   magic-line + RFC 822 headers + `--- log ---` + `--- body ---` form. Each
   tool embarks its own parser; each spec is normative for its own files
   only. At two instances the three-section grammar becomes extractable as a
   one-page mini-spec both specs *cite* (the RFC 822 move) -- when needed,
   not before. Arbitrated 2026-08-26: deferred until a third-party
   implementer exists; the shared parser is the anti-drift mechanism
   meanwhile.
2. **References**, opaque strings. dyne gets repeatable `Closes:` /
   `Ref:` headers whose value grammar is "non-empty line"; `0042` is an
   opaque token that dyne never resolves. Precedent inside erg itself:
   references are URI-references resolved optimistically (0253), and
   `Closed: merged as MR 151` is already free text on the erg side (the
   style becomes `merged as <short-sha>` under the no-numbering
   settlement; the opacity precedent stands either way). The
   cross-pointers ride in fields each tool already treats as opaque -- the
   sign the cut is right.
3. **Behavior**, hooks: exec, not import. `.dynerc`:

       [hooks]
       pre-merge = erg close {closes} "merged as {sha}"

   erg is already hook-shaped, with one precision to respect: `erg close`
   is a four-step operation, idempotent but not atomic -- it edits the
   named ticket, strips `Blocked-by:` references from the other open
   tickets, and moves the file into `tickets/closed/` -- and it neither
   commits nor stages any of it (no git calls in close.go). No git side
   effects to fight is exactly what a composable hook wants, but the
   caller must stage the whole store recursively (`add -A`): a rename
   staged apart from its edit silently carries the pre-edit blob.

## Implementation scheme (inherited)

dyne replicates erg's four documentation layers (maintainer pointer,
2026-08-17). Each layer carries its own anti-drift mechanism and its own
offline guarantee; with one codebase the machinery is shared, the
artifacts stay per-tool:

1. **PEP for the why** -- `pep-dyne-v1.md` beside `pep-erg-v1.md`, written
   before code, one numbered design choice per section (Choice /
   Rationale / Alternatives considered). This ticket's body is its seed.
   The three-scope authority model comes with it: the spec is normative
   for files, the binary for the spec's text, the PEP arbitrates intent.
2. **Manual generated from source** -- `make docs` gains a second line:
   `dyne --help --all > docs/dyne-manual.md`. The manual cannot drift
   from the binary because it is the binary's own help output.
3. **Commands discoverable by CLI** -- `dyne --help --all`,
   `dyne COMMAND --help`, `dyne spec` (the `%dyne` spec embedded and
   served on demand), `dyne integration` (the composition seam --
   hooks, opaque `Closes:` refs -- served on demand). A disconnected
   agent needs no doc site: the binary carries its documentation.
4. **Agentic guide** -- `merges/AGENTS.md` embedded as an asset, deployed by
   `dyne init`, covered by the same self-coherence guard (CI fails
   when the deployed copy drifts from the embedded source).

The seven invariants transfer intact: never lose data (a review thread
is data); agnostic (a hand edit always wins); offline (dyne shells
out to git and carries no network code); standalone (one static binary
plus POSIX, zero third-party dependencies); stateless (DAG-as-state IS
this invariant -- erg removed pending/claimed in 0143/0144, and `%dyne`
refuses a Status field for the same reason); fast (invoked, never
resident); small (the committed binary travels with every clone, so the
size ratchet covers the blob shipped in `merges/`). Separately, the
infrastructure-class security posture (docs/audit-infrastructure-class.md)
comes with any embarked binary from day one: reproducible offline
builds, signed tags.

The repo's feature policy applies too: a feature is pulled by verified
empirical need, never pushed from a wishlist. The MR system's evidence
base is the maintainer's harness failure log -- the erg-pr-merge bounce
recovery recipes, the cross-PR collision scans and their blind-scan
trap, the forge-parsing traps: measured pain, not symmetry. Phase 1
stays the smallest correct system that closes the recorded pain.

## Hard points

- **"Main is read-only"**: today the forge holds the gate (real ACL).
  Sovereign version: a pre-receive hook on one's own bare remote refuses any
  push to main that is not the merge of an APPROVED MR. Without one's own
  remote the rule degrades to convention + CI -- to be said plainly.
- **CI**: who executes? The biggest missing piece, and separable: a local
  runner watching the remote and writing `ci pass` entries. Phase 3.
- **Enumeration of open MRs** is as fresh as the last fetch. Same regime as
  tickets spread across branches; acceptable, to document.
- **Cross-repo coordination** stays on forge issues (existing convention).

## Prior art

Email + patchwork (the original local-MR system; the kernel has run on it
for thirty years); Gerrit (dedicated refs, heavy server); git-appraise (the
closest cousin: serverless, but stores everything in invisible git notes);
git-bug (CRDTs, not hand-editable); Radicle (full P2P stack); Fossil (leaves
git). The niche erg occupies for tickets -- files in the tree, convention
over machinery, state read from the DAG -- is empty for MRs.

## Migration path

1. `dyne` alone: format + `open/show/list/check`; the forge stays the
   authority; the `.dyne` file generates the PR body (outbound bridge).
2. Authority flips: local verdicts and `dyne merge`, the `erg close`
   hook wired, the forge mirrors.
3. Sovereign remote: pre-receive gate + CI runner.

The pilot gains a test fusion could not offer: mount dyne **on a repo
without erg**, proving the orthogonality is not decorative.

## Open decisions (maintainer arbitration)

All settled (2026-08-17 and 2026-08-26 sessions):

- [x] (c) implementation -- settled 2026-08-17: one codebase, two specs
      (see verdict above).
- [x] Grammar mini-spec -- settled 2026-08-26: deferred until a
      third-party implementer exists (verified-empirical-need bar); the
      shared parser removes the drift risk meanwhile.
- [x] Log home -- settled 2026-08-26: inside the `.dyne` file. One file per
      MR, erg's exact three-section shape; log = one-liners, body = the
      discussion (prose; opening post is the description);
      `merge=union` on `merges/*.dyne` from day one; `check` hard-fails
      duplicate headers; prose is append-only by convention.
- [x] Archive numbering -- settled 2026-08-26: no. The merge commit SHA
      is the MR's permanent name; hooks receive `{sha}`; citation style
      `merged as <short-sha>`.
- [x] Name and invocation -- settled 2026-08-26, superseding the same
      day's earlier `git mr` ruling: the tool is **dyne**, the CGS unit
      of force (erg = dyne x cm: an MR is a force applied to main; when
      main moves, the work -- the erg -- is done). Same register as erg,
      kinship without dependency: a repo adopting only dyne sees a
      quirky physics name, not another tool's brand. Standalone binary
      `dyne`, mirroring `erg` (the myrepos collision that motivated the
      `git mr` invocation is moot); extension `.dyne`; magic `%dyne
      0.1`; config `.dynerc`; store `merges/` (names the process, as
      `tickets/` does -- the store needn't be the object's plural);
      blob at `merges/dyne`. Also weighed: `bid` (exact plain-English
      semantics, but commercial flavor, no erg kinship) and `darn` (the
      patch woven into the fabric, but a permanent mild-oath homonym).

## Exit criteria

- [x] Maintainer arbitrates the open decisions above (done 2026-08-26)
- [x] Phase-1 implementation tickets filed: 0271 (PEP) -> 0272 (spec) ->
      0273 (binary + open/show/list/check) -> 0274 (init, embedded docs,
